-- title:
--   Cost of a Lipschitz cutoff in the kernel energy
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$ and $0 < \alpha < 2$. Here $\Gamma$ is a *configuration*: a map assigning to each point $x$ a double cone
--   $\Gamma(x) = V(v,\theta) = \{h \neq 0 : \angle(v,h) < \theta \text{ or } \angle(v,h) > \pi-\theta\}$
--   with unit axis $v$ and apex angle $\theta \in (0,\pi/2]$, and $V^\Gamma[x] = x + \Gamma(x)$ is the cone
--   translated to $x$. The hypothesis `KernelBounds` is assumption (2): $\Lambda \ge 1$, $k$ is symmetric, and
--   $$\Lambda^{-1}\bigl(\mathbf 1_{V^\Gamma[x]}(y)+\mathbf 1_{V^\Gamma[y]}(x)\bigr)\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}
--   \;\le\; k(x,y)\;\le\;\Lambda\,\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}.$$
--
--   Fix a centre $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and radii $R < R'$, and let
--   $$\chi(x) \;=\; \min\Bigl(1,\ \max\Bigl(0,\ \frac{R'-\|x-x_0\|}{R'-R}\Bigr)\Bigr)$$
--   be the radial Lipschitz cutoff: $\chi \equiv 1$ on $B_R(x_0)$, $\chi \equiv 0$ outside $B_{R'}(x_0)$, and $\chi$ interpolates linearly in between. Let $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be measurable and assume $k$ is measurable as a function of the pair. Put
--   $$I \;=\; \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\min\Bigl(\frac{\|u\|^2}{(R'-R)^2},\,1\Bigr)\,\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}\,du .$$
--   Then the energy of the truncated function $\chi f$ over the whole space satisfies
--   $$|\chi f|^2_{H_k(\mathbb{R}^d)} \;\le\; 2\,|f|^2_{H_k(B_{R'}(x_0))} \;+\; 4\,\Lambda\, I \int_{B_{R'}(x_0)} f(x)^2\,dx ,$$
--   where $|f|^2_{H_k(\Omega)} = \int_{\Omega\times\Omega}(f(y)-f(x))^2 k(x,y)\,d(x,y)$.
--
--   The inequality is the bridge between a statement on a ball and a statement on all of $\mathbb{R}^d$: multiplying by $\chi$ produces a function agreeing with $f$ on the inner ball and supported in the outer one, at the price of the kernel energy on the outer ball plus an $L^2$ term on the outer ball. Crucially the right-hand side never sees $f$ outside $B_{R'}(x_0)$, so extending $\chi f$ by zero is legitimate — a sharp cutoff would not have this property. The factor $I$ is finite exactly because $0 < \alpha < 2$ makes the quadratic cutoff factor absorb the singularity of $\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}$ at the origin.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Values lie in `ℝ≥0∞` and all integrals are lower Lebesgue integrals; no finiteness of any side is assumed. In the Lean statement the term $4\Lambda I \int_{B_{R'}} f^2$ appears as two syntactically identical summands $2\bigl(\Lambda I \int_{B_{R'}} f^2\bigr)$. Only $R < R'$ is required — no positivity of $R$ — and the balls are open balls.
